A collection loop visits values from a Moth collection. By default it walks
from the first item to the last.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    names = {"Priya", "Rob", "Emmy"}

    loop names |name, index|:
        io.line([: [index]: [name]])
    ;
]

### Forms of collection loops

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop items:
        ~count += 1
    ;

    loop items |item|:
        inspect(item)
    ;

    loop items by step:
        ~count += 1
    ;

    loop items by step |item|:
        inspect(item)
    ;

    loop items by step |item, index|:
        inspect(item)
    ;
]

### Collection loop contract

- The source expression must have a collection type.
- Collection loops do not use a general iterable protocol.
- Hash maps are not collection-loop sources.
- Bindings are optional.
- The first binding receives the current item and has the collection's element type.
- The optional second binding receives the actual source index of the current item and has type `I32`. A stepped loop visits source indexes such as `0`, `2` and `4`, not ordinal positions.
- Binding names are written inside one `|...|` suffix.
- A binding list may contain one or two names.
- An empty binding list, a third binding or duplicate binding names are invalid.
- Loop bindings are immutable body-local names.
- Binding an unresolved whole-number item pins its integer family, including whole-number leaves in a known nested item shape. Later integer receivers can still select its width, but float, decimal and Byte receivers or peers require an explicit cast.
- A binding name must not collide with another visible name.
- An empty collection with a non-zero step runs the body zero times.

### Collection loop evaluation order

The source expression is evaluated once before iteration begins. The collection
length is captured at the same point. An explicit step is then evaluated once.

The step is a signed fixed-width integer (`I8`, `I16`, `I32` or `I64`) and keeps
its own type. An omitted step is `+1` with type `I32`, and an untyped
whole-number literal step also defaults to `I32`. A step whose type is unsigned,
binary-float, `Byte` or `Dec` is rejected. A zero step is invalid.

A positive step starts at index `0` and advances by the step while the index is
below the captured length. A negative step starts at the last source index and
advances by the signed step while the index remains at or above `0`. An overshoot
ends the loop without constructing an invalid index. With a non-zero step, an
empty source visits nothing.

`continue` advances by the step. `break` exits the loop.

A literal zero step is rejected during compilation with `MOTH-SYNTAX-0029`. A
dynamic zero step fails with built-in error code 306 (`InvalidLoopStep`) before
the empty-source check.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    stride_items = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}

    loop stride_items by 2 |item, index|:
        io.line([: [index]: [item]])
    ;

    loop stride_items by -2 |item, index|:
        io.line([: [index]: [item]])
    ;
]

The first loop visits `10` at index `0`, `30` at index `2` and `50` at index
`4`. The second loop visits `50` at index `4`, `30` at index `2` and `10` at
index `0`.

### Removed syntax

The old `in` form is not valid.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop item in items:
        inspect(item)
    ;
]

Write the collection source first, then any `by` step, then bindings after it:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop items by 2 |item|:
        inspect(item)
    ;
]

@../progress (Progress) records target support, including current HTML-Wasm
collection limits, separately from these collection semantics.
